  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nAfternoon highs look to generally warm into the 80s each afternoon \nof the Long Term period, with 70s forecast for areas in and near the \nhigher terrain. Overnight lows fall to the mid 50s to lower 60s each \nnight. Tuesday, mid level ridging remains our primary weather maker. \nLow (10-30%) rain chances will be confined to the Davis Mountains \nTuesday afternoon. A change from previous forecast packages, current \nmodels have an upper level disturbance coming out of\nnorthwestern Mexico Wednesday, headed east. Rain chances arrive \nfirst to our southwesternmost counties Wednesday morning. \nWednesday afternoon, 10-50% chances of isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms spread east to encompass most areas west of the \nPecos River. This activity may persist over western portions of \nour area through Wednesday evening. Long range models have this \nshortwave becoming an upper low somewhere near our area Thursday. \nMeanwhile, a surface trough is progged to extend over our region, \naiding to increase rain chances almost areawide Thursday and \nFriday. There currently remains some uncertainty regarding exactly\nwhere this low will develop, its motion, and how much moisture \nwill be available once it materializes. We will have to see how \nthis system takes shape going forward. At the very least, lower \nheights associated with the low will shave a few degrees off of \ntemperatures during the latter half of the week.",
